          <span class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-teal block mb-2">01 &bull; Overview &amp; Abstract</span>
          <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-bold text-charcoal mb-6">Thesis Abstract</h2>
          
          <div class="space-y-4 text-base text-charcoal/90 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              This thesis investigates the social construction of femininity through the lens of consumer culture, examining how contemporary market segmentation strategies and gender-based pricing practices—commonly discussed under the umbrella term of the &ldquo;pink tax&rdquo;—function as part of a broader gendered marketplace through which femininity is made visible, desirable, and economically costly.
            </p>
            <p>
              Drawing on Judith Butler's theory of gender performativity, Peter Berger and Thomas Luckmann's framework of the social construction of reality, and materialist feminist accounts of the relationship between gender, consumption, and capital accumulation, this study analyses the mechanisms by which commercial culture generates, sustains, and naturalises femininity as a category simultaneously of identity and of consumption.
            </p>
            <p>
              The empirical component consists of ten semi-structured qualitative interviews conducted with two groups: five women without children and five mothers, all residing in Bucharest, Romania. The thesis situates these dynamics within postsocialist Romania, where consumerism acquired particular symbolic and social weight following the transition from state socialism, and where gendered consumption intersects with class position and social reproduction.
            </p>
          </div>

