            Structured full-text summary of Chapters 1–7 and Bibliography (clean academic edition).
          </p>
        </div>

        <button onclick="toggleAllChapters()" id="expand-chapters-btn" class="px-4 py-2 rounded-xl text-xs font-semibold bg-white border border-stone-300 text-charcoal hover:bg-stone-100 transition-colors self-start cursor-pointer no-print">
          Expand All Chapters
        </button>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-3" id="chapters-accordion">
        
        <!-- Chapter 1 -->
        <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-stone-200 overflow-hidden">
          <button onclick="toggleChapter(1)" class="w-full px-6 py-4 text-left flex items-center justify-between gap-4 hover:bg-stone-50 transition-colors cursor-pointer">
            <div>
              <span class="text-xs font-bold text-teal uppercase tracking-wider">Chapter 1</span>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-charcoal">Introduction</h3>
            </div>
            <span id="icon-ch-1" class="text-xl font-light text-muted">+</span>
          </button>
          <div id="body-ch-1" class="chapter-body hidden px-6 pb-6 pt-2 text-sm text-charcoal/90 leading-relaxed space-y-3 border-t border-stone-100">
            <p>
              Walk into any Romanian supermarket and a familiar scene presents itself: in the personal care aisle, two versions of functionally the same product sit side by side—one in dark packaging, one in pink—and the pink one costs more. In the toy section, a grey-and-yellow plastic &ldquo;workshop&rdquo; kitchen sits across from an identical pink floral kitchen priced ten lei higher.
            </p>
            <p>
              This thesis addresses why women who are aware and critical of gender-based marketing continue to reproduce feminised consumption practices daily. It introduces <strong>performative complicity</strong> to describe knowledgeable participants in a commercial system whose logic they partially contest yet remain socially, materially, and relationally invested in reproducing—situated within postsocialist urban Romania following the post-1989 expansion of Western consumer goods.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Chapter 2 -->
        <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-stone-200 overflow-hidden">
          <button onclick="toggleChapter(2)" class="w-full px-6 py-4 text-left flex items-center justify-between gap-4 hover:bg-stone-50 transition-colors cursor-pointer">
            <div>
              <span class="text-xs font-bold text-teal uppercase tracking-wider">Chapter 2</span>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-charcoal">Literature Review and Theoretical Framework</h3>
            </div>
            <span id="icon-ch-2" class="text-xl font-light text-muted">+</span>
          </button>
          <div id="body-ch-2" class="chapter-body hidden px-6 pb-6 pt-2 text-sm text-charcoal/90 leading-relaxed space-y-3 border-t border-stone-100">
            <p>
              <strong>2.1–2.2 Social Construction &amp; Gender Performativity:</strong> Synthesises Berger &amp; Luckmann (1966), West &amp; Zimmerman's (1987) &ldquo;doing gender,&rdquo; and Judith Butler's (1990) theory that gender is enacted through repeated, socially enforced performances rather than expressing a biological essence.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>2.3–2.5 Political Economy, Segmentation &amp; The Pink Tax:</strong> Integrates Hochschild (1983), Harvey (2003), Federici (2004), Wolf (1991), and McRobbie (2009) to show how beauty and personal care markets monetise reproductive self-presentation labour. Reviews empirical pink tax studies (NYC DCA, 2015; European Parliament B&ouml;hmer &amp; Saraceno, 2020; Guittar et al., 2022; Moshary et al., 2023) alongside original March 2026 retail data from Jumbo Romania showing 10%–35% premiums.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>2.6–2.7 Toys &amp; Postsocialist Romania:</strong> Examines early gender socialisation in toy markets (Blakemore &amp; Centers, 2005; Orenstein, 2011; Sweet, 2013) and Romania's post-1989 transition (Verdery, 1996; Gr&uuml;nberg, 2000; Dragolea, 2012), where Western consumer femininity became intertwined with modernity and middle-class distinction.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Chapter 3 -->
        <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-stone-200 overflow-hidden">
          <button onclick="toggleChapter(3)" class="w-full px-6 py-4 text-left flex items-center justify-between gap-4 hover:bg-stone-50 transition-colors cursor-pointer">
            <div>
              <span class="text-xs font-bold text-teal uppercase tracking-wider">Chapter 3</span>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-charcoal">Methodology</h3>
            </div>
            <span id="icon-ch-3" class="text-xl font-light text-muted">+</span>
          </button>
          <div id="body-ch-3" class="chapter-body hidden px-6 pb-6 pt-2 text-sm text-charcoal/90 leading-relaxed space-y-3 border-t border-stone-100">
            <p>
              Employs a constructionist qualitative design using semi-structured interviews conducted in Bucharest between February and April 2026 (42–78 minutes each; mean ~58 minutes). Purposive sampling recruited 10 participants across two equal groups: Group A (5 women without children, ages 24–35) and Group B (5 mothers of children under 12, ages 34–45).
            </p>
            <p>
              Verbatim transcripts were analysed via Braun and Clarke's (2006) six-stage thematic analysis combining deductive theoretical codes and inductive codes. Full informed consent and pseudonymisation were maintained in accordance with University of Bucharest ethical standards.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Chapter 4 -->
        <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-stone-200 overflow-hidden">
          <button onclick="toggleChapter(4)" class="w-full px-6 py-4 text-left flex items-center justify-between gap-4 hover:bg-stone-50 transition-colors cursor-pointer">
            <div>
              <span class="text-xs font-bold text-rose uppercase tracking-wider">Chapter 4</span>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-charcoal">The Gendered Marketplace: Awareness, Pricing, and Normalisation</h3>
            </div>
            <span id="icon-ch-4" class="text-xl font-light text-muted">+</span>
          </button>
          <div id="body-ch-4" class="chapter-body hidden px-6 pb-6 pt-2 text-sm text-charcoal/90 leading-relaxed space-y-3 border-t border-stone-100">
            <p>
              Demonstrates how critical awareness of gendered price differentials coexists with continued purchasing. Participants either experience gendered pricing as naturalised common sense (Bourdieu's <em>doxa</em>) or find deliberate resistance (such as buying men's razors) undermined by household/relational norms and inelastic categories like menstrual hygiene products.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Chapter 5 -->
        <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-stone-200 overflow-hidden">
          <button onclick="toggleChapter(5)" class="w-full px-6 py-4 text-left flex items-center justify-between gap-4 hover:bg-stone-50 transition-colors cursor-pointer">
            <div>
              <span class="text-xs font-bold text-rose uppercase tracking-wider">Chapter 5</span>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-charcoal">Performing Femininity: Consumption as Identity Work</h3>
            </div>
            <span id="icon-ch-5" class="text-xl font-light text-muted">+</span>
          </button>
          <div id="body-ch-5" class="chapter-body hidden px-6 pb-6 pt-2 text-sm text-charcoal/90 leading-relaxed space-y-3 border-t border-stone-100">
            <p>
              Explores how daily skincare, scent, and haircare routines operate as identity work producing the effect of a coherent feminine self. Analyses &ldquo;The Pleasure Problem&rdquo;: participants derive genuine sensory enjoyment from feminised rituals while navigating adolescent and adult peer policing that enforces brand conformity.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Chapter 6 -->
        <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-stone-200 overflow-hidden">
          <button onclick="toggleChapter(6)" class="w-full px-6 py-4 text-left flex items-center justify-between gap-4 hover:bg-stone-50 transition-colors cursor-pointer">
            <div>
              <span class="text-xs font-bold text-rose uppercase tracking-wider">Chapter 6</span>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-charcoal">Mothers, Toys, and the Socialisation of Gendered Consumption</h3>
            </div>
            <span id="icon-ch-6" class="text-xl font-light text-muted">+</span>
          </button>
          <div id="body-ch-6" class="chapter-body hidden px-6 pb-6 pt-2 text-sm text-charcoal/90 leading-relaxed space-y-3 border-t border-stone-100">
            <p>
              Examines mothers' encounters with &ldquo;the pink aisle&rdquo; and price premiums on girls' toys—including +10% markups on pink toy kitchens versus &ldquo;workshops&rdquo; and +29% markups on floral-packaged &ldquo;girls' science kits.&rdquo; Highlights intergenerational counter-socialisation and retail literacy as forms of feminist mothering (O'Reilly, 2004).
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Chapter 7 & Bibliography -->
        <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-stone-200 overflow-hidden">
          <button onclick="toggleChapter(7)" class="w-full px-6 py-4 text-left flex items-center justify-between gap-4 hover:bg-stone-50 transition-colors cursor-pointer">
            <div>
              <span class="text-xs font-bold text-charcoal uppercase tracking-wider">Chapter 7 &amp; References</span>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-charcoal">Conclusions, Policy Implications &amp; Core Bibliography</h3>
            </div>
            <span id="icon-ch-7" class="text-xl font-light text-muted">+</span>
          </button>
          <div id="body-ch-7" class="chapter-body hidden px-6 pb-6 pt-2 text-sm text-charcoal/90 leading-relaxed space-y-3 border-t border-stone-100">
            <p>
              Synthesises the thesis's theoretical contributions, outlines VAT and retail transparency policy recommendations, and notes directions for future quantitative and cross-class research.
            </p>
            <div class="pt-3 border-t border-stone-100 text-xs text-muted space-y-1.5">
              <strong class="text-charcoal block mb-1">Selected Key References:</strong>
              <p>&bull; Berger, P. L., &amp; Luckmann, T. (1966). <em>The social construction of reality</em>. Doubleday.</p>
              <p>&bull; B&ouml;hmer, S., &amp; Saraceno, C. (2020). <em>The price of being a woman: The gender price differential in the EU</em>. European Parliament Research Service.</p>
